{
  "result": "There is a particular blue that belongs only to the hour before the sun fully arrives, when the sky seems to hold its breath. I have come to love that blue more than any other, because it is unclaimed time. The world is still deciding whether to be kind or merely busy, and for a few minutes, the scales can tip either way.\n\nI make coffee with the kind of attention usually reserved for rituals. The grind, the pour, the first bloom of steam against the window. It is not really about caffeine; it is about having something small and reliable to begin with. The day ahead will contain emails, unexpected silences, small disappointments, perhaps a small joy. But first, there is this cup, this chair, this light.\n\nI think often about how we are stitched together by these minor repetitions. The way a neighbor nods at the same hour each morning. The way a dog waits by the same patch of fence. The way someone we love leaves a coffee mug in the same place on the counter, and how that place, after a while, becomes a kind of absence when they are gone.\n\nWe are told to seek meaning in grand gestures, in achievements, in destinations. But I am less and less convinced. Meaning seems to me now like dust motes in a slant of light: ordinary, countless, visible only if you stop long enough to notice. The blue hour fades. The kettle cools. The street below begins its ordinary song. I finish my cup, and I carry that small, ordinary grace into whatever comes next.",
